"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[438],{438:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"anatomy-test-2","slug":"anatomy","subject":"Anatomy","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Identify the labelled structure","options":["Internal capsule","Caudate nucleus","Globus pallidus","Thalamus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Internal capsule\\n\\nThe labelled structure is the Internal capsule.\\n\\nThe internal capsule is a component of the white matter of the brain. They contain projection fibers. It can be divided into the following parts:\\n\\n• anterior limb\\n• genu\\n• posterior limb\\n• retrolenticular/ retrolentiform portion\\n• sublenticular/ sublentiform portion\\n\\nThe anterior limb of the internal capsule carries the fronto-pontine fibers, while the genu carries the corticonuclear tract.\\n\\nThe posterior limb of the internal capsule carries cortico-spinal motor fibers in the anterior part. The posterior part of the posterior limb of the internal capsule carries sensory fibers.\\n\\nThe retrolenticular part contains the parietopontine, occipitopontine and occipitotectal fibres and the sublenticular part contains temporopontine and some parietopontine fibres.\\n\\nThe basal ganglia constitutes accessory motor system that functions in close association with the cerebral cortex and corticospinal motor control system. On each side of the brain, these ganglia consist of the caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and subthalamic nucleus.\\n\\nNomenclature of basal ganglia:\\n\\n• Corpus striatum: Caudate nucleus + putamen+ globus pallidus\\n• Lentiform nucleus: Putamen + globus pallidus\\n• Neostriatum: Caudate nucleus + putamen\\n\\nThe internal capsule separates the thalamus and the caudate nucleus from the lentiform nucleus. The caudate nucleus is a C-shaped mass of grey matter. It is divided into a head, body, and tail. Degeneration of the caudate nucleus is seen in Huntington\'s disease.\\n\\nBoth the Putamen and external capsule lie lateral to globus pallidus. It receives fibers from the striatum and subthalamic nuclei. It acts as the main output of the basal ganglia to the thalamus and superior colliculus. The substantia nigra is seen in each cerebral peduncle of the midbrain located deep to the crus cerebri. The levels of dopamine in the substantia nigra and striata are reduced in parkinsonism disease.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/20c94a10fa6b91278dca.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fcf6fa567697122668a4.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4d3a7464c3fc4fa67600.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/53da8a630f5e7a1244e4.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/67d349bd37f84c07c45b.jpg"}]},{"n":2,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Which of the following is not a hybrid muscle?","options":["2","1","4","3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 3\\n\\nThe sternohyoid\xa0muscle marked as \'3\' is not a hybrid muscle. It is innervated only by the anterior rami of spinal nerves C1-C3 (via the ansa cervicalis)\\n\\nHybrid muscles are the muscles that are supplied by two different nerves with different root values.\\n\\nOther muscles in the given image are:\\n\\n1 - Trapezius\\n\\n2 - Sternocleidomastoid\\n\\n4 - Digastric muscle (anterior belly)\\n\\nHybrid muscles of head and neck:\\n\\n• Digastric - Anterior belly is supplied by the nerve to the mylohyoid and posterior belly is supplied by the facial nerve.\\n• Trapezius and sternocleidomastoid (SCM) - Motor supply is by the spinal accessory nerve and the proprioception is by ventral rami of cervical nerves (C2, C3 for SCM and C3, C4 for trapezius).","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/95dce64e65c15f6e7012.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/30e6b3caa7ad8b2eeb99.jpg"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Which of the following gives rise to the structure marked in the given image of the liver?","options":["Septum transversum","Dorsal mesogastrium","Gastrophrenic ligament","Splanchnopleuric lateral plate mesoderm"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Septum transversum\\n\\nThe marked structure is the falciform ligament of the liver. It is an embryological derivative of ventral mesentery, which itself arises from septum transversum.\\n\\nSeptum transversum is the undifferentiated lateral plate mesoderm at the cranial end of the embryo. It gives rise to:\\n\\n• Central tendon of diaphragm\\n• Fibrous pericardium\\n• Liver stroma\\n• Ventral mesogastrium\\n\\nAs the liver grows into the mesenchyme of the septum transversum, ventral mesentery is divided into two parts:\\n\\n1. Lesser omentum, which\xa0extends\xa0from the lower portion of the esophagus, the stomach, and the upper portion of the duodenum to the liver (hepato-gastric and hepato-duodenal ligaments)\\n2. Ligaments of the liver: all ligaments of the liver except ligamentum teres are derived from the ventral mesentery. This includes\xa0falciform ligament, right and left triangular ligaments, and coronary ligaments.\\n\\nDorsal mesentery\xa0connects the abdominal organs to the posterior abdominal wall. It runs from the\xa0lower end of the esophagus to the cloacal region of the hindgut. Depending on the abdominal organ to which it is attached, it has multiple regions: dorsal mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper (to the small intestine), mesocolon, mesoappendix, mesosigmoid, and mesorectum. The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to greater omentum, gastrosplenic ligament, lienorenal ligament, and gastrophrenic ligament.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/dd663b2007432b61fd44.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b099cc67acffc8a63879.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c735c9874a412e733d0a.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/391136d859de3a1befce.jpg"}]},{"n":4,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"The marked area in the given image is supplied by which dermatome?","options":["T11","T10","T9","T8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. T10\\n\\nThe marked dermatome which includes the umbilicus is supplied by T10.\\n\\nThe dermatomal distribution of the abdominal region is as follows:\\n\\n• The skin from the xiphoid process to just above the umbilicus is supplied by T7-T9.\\n• The skin around the umbilicus is supplied by T10.\\n• The skin below the umbilicus until the suprapubic region is supplied by T11, T12.\\n• The skin around genitalia and upper medial thigh is supplied by L1.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4c868c6187b409ec58e0.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/db52d1bda4ebdda3b42c.jpg"}]},{"n":5,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 2-year-old girl playing with small magnets suddenly develops breathlessness. Chest X-ray shows an inhaled foreign body in one of the bronchi. Where does the vein supplying the most likely site of obstruction drain into?","options":["Right brachiocephalic vein","Right subclavian vein","Azygous vein","Accessory hemiazygous vein"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Azygous vein\\n\\nIn the given case scenario of an inhaled foreign body, it is most likely to lodge into the right bronchus.\xa0The right bronchial veins drain to the azygos vein.\\n\\nLeft bronchial veins drain into the accessory hemiazygos vein\xa0or\xa0left superior intercostal vein.\\n\\nClinical significance:\\n\\nThe\xa0right principal bronchus\xa0is\xa0wider, shorter, and more vertical. This is why an inhaled foreign body is more likely to lodge there. Also, the tracheal bifurcation directs the foreign body to the right lung","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2a35c5d49f8e02526185.jpg"}]},{"n":6,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"The type of joint involved in the following movement of the neck is?","options":["Pivot joint","Ellipsoid joint","Hinge joint","Ball-and-socket joint"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Pivot joint\\n\\nThe joint for side-to-side movements in the neck is the atlanto-axial joint which is a type of pivot joint.\\n\\nMovement in the atlanto-axial joints are simultaneous and consists almost exclusively of rotation around the axis, which helps a person look to the right and left.\\n\\n• The normal range of atlanto-axial rotation is about 40\xb0.\\n• The orientation and shape of the atlanto-occipital joints allow mainly for flexion–extension, which has a total range of about 15\xb0.\\n\\nPivot joint:\\n\\nIn pivot joint, one bone rotates in relation to another bone.\\n\\nThis is a uniaxial joint, and thus rotation is the only motion allowed at a pivot joint.\\n\\nEg:\\n\\n• Atlantoaxial joint: the first cervical (C1) vertebra (atlas) rotates around the dens, the upward projection from the second cervical (C2) vertebra (axis). This allows the head to rotate from side to side as when shaking the head “no.”\\n• The proximal radioulnar joint is a pivot joint formed by the head of the radius and its articulation with the ulna. This joint allows for the radius to rotate along its length during pronation and supination movements of the forearm.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/7414ffd66831a712cf84.png"]},{"n":7,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Arrange the following in the correct order from proximal to distal:\\n\\n1. Alveolar duct\\n\\n2. Lobar bronchi\\n\\n3. Terminal bronchiole\\n\\n4. Alveolar sac\\n\\n5. Segmental bronchi\\n\\n6. Respiratory bronchiole","options":["2→5→6→3→1→4","5→2→3→1→6→4","5→2→6→1→3→4","2→5→3→6→1→4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 2→5→3→6→1→4\\n\\nThe following is a sequence of the intrapulmonary anatomy from proximal to distal:\xa0Trachea → Main Bronchi → Lobar Bronchi → Segmental Bronchi → Terminal Bronchiole → Respiratory Bronchiole → Alveolar Duct → Alveolar Sac → Alveoli\\n\\nAccording to Weibel’s model, the respiratory tract is divided into 23 generations, consisting of conducting (upper 16 generations) and respiratory zones (lower 7 generations). It begins at the trachea (designated as zero) and ends with alveoli (last generation).\\n\\nThe epithelial layer transitions from a pseudostratified columnar layer in the upper airways (trachea, bronchi) with submucosal secretory glands to a cuboidal epithelium (bronchioles), and finally, to a flat squamous epithelium in the alveoli, facilitating efficient gas exchange in the respiratory zone.\\n\\nAs we progress downward, the following changes occur in the structure of the airways:\\n\\n• The number of mucus-secreting cells/secretory glands decreases.\\n• The amount of cartilage in the airway wall decreases.\\n• The quantity of smooth muscles increases from trachea to terminal bronchioles but decreases from respiratory bronchioles to alveoli.\\n• The amount of dead space decreases.\\n• The number of cilia decreases such that they are absent in alveoli.\\n• The surface area and volume progressively increase, facilitating easy gas exchange.\\n• The number of flat squamous cells (Type 1 pneumocytes) increases.\\n\\nThe diagram given below further reiterates the above concept","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fb223a3ddd71f4aa1751.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e822dadf3f05b2628b71.jpg"}]},{"n":8,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A patient with traumatic injury to the skull is diagnosed to have an epidural hematoma. The area of impact is at the pterion, causing damage to the underlying artery. Which of the following foramen does this artery traverse through?","options":["1","4","3","2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 3\\n\\nTraumatic injury to the pterion can damage the underlying anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery.\xa0The middle meningeal artery enters the middle cranial fossa through the foramen spinosum, labeled as 3 in the image.\\n\\nThe structures passing through the foramen spinosum are:\\n\\n• Middle meningeal artery\\n• Nervus spinosus\\n• Middle meningeal vein\\n\\nThe pterion is an H-shaped sutural junction that overlies the anterior\xa0branch of the\xa0middle meningeal artery\xa0and the\xa0lateral fissure\xa0of the\xa0cerebral hemisphere. The bone in this area is thin and any injury to this area may lead to rupture of the middle meningeal artery leading to\xa0extradural hematoma.\\n\\nThe four bones forming the pterion are:\\n\\n• Frontal bone\\n• Squamous part of the temporal bone\\n• Greater wing of the sphenoid bone\\n• Parietal bone\\n\\nThe foramina marked in the image are:\\n\\n1 - Foramen rotundum\\n2 - Foramen ovale\\n3 - Foramen spinosum\\n4 - Jugular foramen","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/0fb2aa06eb096d60aeed.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f78379ed1cc7a850cd84.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3276b81ca33c99050b86.jpg"}]},{"n":9,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"An intern was asked to give an intravenous injection to a vein in the cubital fossa. All of the following are true about the cubital fossa except:","options":["Median cubital vein forms the roof","Musculocutaneous nerve is one of the contents","Radial nerve enters the fossa laterally","Brachioradialis forms the lateral boundary"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Musculocutaneous nerve is one of the contents\\n\\nThe musculocutaneous nerve is not a content of the cubital fossa.\\n\\nThe cubital fossa is a triangular depression present in the anterior side of the elbow joint. The boundaries of the cubital fossa include:\\n\\n• Medially: Lateral border of the pronator teres\\n• Laterally: Medial border of the brachioradialis (option A)\\n• Base: Proximally by an imaginary line joining the epicondyles of the humerus\\n• Apex (pointed downward): Joining of the lateral and medial boundaries.\\n• Roof: Skin, superficial fascia containing median cubital vein (option D) joining the cephalic and basilic veins, lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm, bicipital aponeurosis, and deep fascia.\\n• Floor: Brachialis and supinator muscles.\\n\\nThe contents of the brachial fossa are (from medial to lateral side):\\n\\n• Median nerve\\n• Brachial artery, terminating into radial and ulnar arteries\\n• Biceps brachii tendon with the bicipital aponeurosis\\n• Radial nerve\xa0enters the fossa laterally (option C) in the groove between the brachialis and brachioradialis\\n\\nClinical significance:\\n\\n• The median cubital vein is the most commonly preferred vein of choice for intravenous injections.\\n• Auscultation of the brachial artery in front of the elbow helps in measuring the blood pressure.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1f256279479280058f24.jpg"}]},{"n":10,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A fresh high school graduate attended an Army recruitment drive but was rejected due to his flat foot. The primary ligament in his foot that is most likely responsible for this condition is the _______.","options":["Spring ligament","Anterior Talo-fibular Ligament","Posterior Talo-fibular Ligament","Deltoid Ligament"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Spring ligament\\n\\nA flat foot is caused due to the involvement of the spring ligament.\\n\\nPes planus or flat foot is caused to due the collapse of the medial longitudinal arch of the foot and can be either congenital or acquired. Its causes include rupture of spring ligament, tarsal coalition, disruption of tibialis posterior tendon, tarsometatarsal arthritis, and hindfoot degenerative or inflammatory arthritis.\\n\\nSpring ligament, also known as the plantar calcaneonavicular ligament, connects the sustentaculum tali to the navicular. It ties the calcaneus to the navicular and maintains the medial longitudinal arch of the foot. It is made up of the superomedial calcaneonavicular portion and the inferior calcaneonavicular portion.\\n\\nIt supports the head of the talus which if fails or is destroyed, leads to separation of the navicular and calcaneus, in turn allowing the talar head, the highest point of the arch, to descend and leading to a flat-foot deformity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: The anterior talofibular ligament is the “ankle sprain ligament” because it is the one that is most often damaged when the ankle is rolled.\\n\\nOption C: The posterior talofibular ligament functions to stabilize the ankle joint and subtalar joint. Injuries are rare to this ligament unless there is an ankle dislocation or subluxation.\\n\\nOption D: The deltoid ligament is extremely strong and stabilizes the inside of the ankle. Tears of this ligament are most common when the ankle undergoes an\xa0unstable ankle fracture.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/78514e5be10b7f646e9e.jpg"}]},{"n":11,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"After removing the parotid gland, the patient presents with sweating on the cheeks while eating. This reflects aberrant innervation of which nerve?","options":["Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Great auricular nerve"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Auriculotemporal nerve\\n\\nThe above symptoms reflect aberrant innervation of the auriculotemporal nerve.\\n\\nThe patient experiencing sweating on cheeks while eating, is the symptom of Frey\'s syndrome or gustatory sweating or auriculotemporal syndrome.\\n\\n• It commonly develops after parotid surgery or any trauma where there is an injury to the parotid capsule.\\n• During the regrowth, an aberrant connection develops between the parasympathetic fibres supplying the\xa0parotid gland\xa0(auriculotemporal nerve) and the\xa0sweat glands. This results in sweating, warmth and redness of the face during the salivary stimulation by the smell or taste of food.\\n• Management -\xa0The symptoms can be treated by\xa0subcutaneous infiltration of purified botulinum toxin into the affected area and use of antiperspirant. Denervation by tympanic neurectomy or auriculotemporal nerve may be useful.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":12,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Match the embryological origin with their derivatives.\\n\\nEmbryological origin\\nDerivatives\\n\\na) Septum transversum\\n\\n1) Hepatogastric ligament\\n\\nb) Dorsal pancreatic bud\\n\\n2) Duct of santorini\\n\\nc) Ventral pancreatic bud\\n\\n3) Kupffer cells\\n\\nd) Ventral mesogastrium\\n4) Gastrophrenic ligament\\n\\ne) Dorsal mesogastrium\\n5) Uncinate process","options":["a-3, b-5, c-2, d-1, e-4","a-3, b-2, c-5, d-1, e-4","a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4, e-5","a-3, b-2, c-5, d-4, e-1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. a-3, b-2, c-5, d-1, e-4\\n\\nAll the above structures are the embryological of the digestive system. The liver bud, ventral pancreatic bud and dorsal pancreatic bud arise from the foregut, ventral mesogastrium arises from the septum transversum and dorsal mesogastrium arises from the lateral plate mesoderm.\\n\\nVentral and dorsal mesogastrium develop into the peritoneal connections in the abdomen which are together called ventral and dorsal mesentery.\\n\\nLiver is developed by the septum transversum and the hepatic bud.\\n\\nVentral and dorsal pancreatic buds give rise to the pancreas and the pancreatic ducts. When the duodenum rotates to the right to form a “C” shape, the ventral pancreatic bud moves dorsally. It moves such that it comes to lie immediately below and behind the dorsal pancreatic bud. Later the parenchyma and the ducts of both the buds fuse. Development of pancreas is as shown in the below image.\\n\\nEmbryological origin of foregut structures\\n\\nDerivatives\\n\\n1) Respiratory bud\\n\\nTrachea, primary bronchial buds\\n\\n2) Liver bud\\n\\nHepatocytes, biliary apparatus\\n\\n3 )Septum transversum\\n\\nKupffer cells, stromal connective tissue\\n\\n4) Ventral pancreatic bud\\n\\nUncinate process, lower head of pancreas, duct of Wirsung\\n\\n5) Dorsal pancreatic bud\\n\\nUpper head of pancreas,neck, body and tail of pancreas, duct of Santorini\\n\\n6) Ventral mesogastrium\\n\\nFalciform ligament, lesser omentum(hepatogastric ligament, hepatoduodenal ligament)\\n\\n7) Dorsal mesogastrium\\n\\nGreater omentum, gastrophrenic ligament, gastrosplenic ligament, lienorenal ligament","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9ede507abf3e23ef6110.jpg"}]},{"n":13,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"An elderly man with atherosclerosis presented with abdominal pain and blood-stained stools. CECT abdomen is suggestive of ischemic colitis at the splenic flexure involving the marginal artery of Drummond. What are the arteries contributing to the formation of this structure?","options":["Ileocolic and left colic arteries","Left colic and sigmoid arteries","Right colic and left colic arteries","Left colic and middle colic arteries"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Left colic and middle colic arteries\\n\\nThe middle and left colic arteries\xa0contribute to the formation of the marginal artery of Drummond at the splenic flexure.\xa0The splenic flexure (Griffith\'s point) is a watershed area of the colon which is more prone to ischemia.\\n\\nThe anastomotic vessel that runs in the mesentery along the inner margin of the colon is called the marginal artery of Drummond. From this artery, numerous small branches are given to the large bowel. It is formed by:\\n\\n• Branches of superior mesenteric artery - middle colic, right colic, and ileocolic arteries\\n• Branches of inferior mesenteric artery - left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries\\n\\nWhen ischemia occurs at Griffith\'s point, the blood supply in this region is augmented by another arterial loop called the inner arterial arc (of Riolan). This arc connects the main trunk of the middle colic artery with the ascending branch of the left colic artery.\\n\\nNote: Sudeck\'s point is another watershed area present at the rectosigmoid junction. The sigmoid artery anastomoses with the superior rectal artery at this site.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/245257484233c02eb199.jpg"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A patient has dry eye and reduced nasal secretions. The location of a lesion might be in the ____.","options":["Superior cervical ganglion","Ciliary ganglion","Otic ganglion","Sphenopalatine ganglion"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Sphenopalatine ganglion\\n\\nIn this patient with\xa0dry eye and reduced nasal secretions, the\xa0lacrimal gland,\xa0and\xa0glands of the nasal mucosa are affected. So the lesion is possibly in the\xa0sphenopalatine ganglion.\\n\\nParasympathetic (secretomotor) supply to the lacrimal gland, glands of the nasal mucosa and palate travel in the nerve of the pterygoid canal i.e, Vidian nerve, and reach the sphenopalatine ganglion (a.k.a Pterygopalatine ganglion\xa0or\xa0nasal ganglion)\xa0where they relay before reaching the target. They also supply the blood vessels of the nose and cause vasodilation.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/38a54202fc43c2923e45.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 24-year-old lady, with a past history of pulmonary tuberculosis, presents to the OPD with complaints of infertility. After further evaluation, you diagnose her with genital tuberculosis. The most common structure involved in this condition is derived from which of the following?","options":["Mesonephric duct","Wolffian duct","Mullerian duct","Urogenital sinus"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Mullerian duct\\n\\nThe most common structure involved in genital tuberculosis are the fallopian tubes which develop from the Mullerian ducts (paramesonephric ducts).\\n\\nThree parts can be recognized in each paramesonephric duct: (1) a cranial vertical portion that opens into the abdominal cavity, (2) a horizontal part that crosses the mesonephric duct, and (3) a caudal vertical part that fuses with its partner from the opposite side.\\n\\nWith the descent of the ovary, the first two parts develop into the fallopian tube, and the caudal parts fuse to form the uterine canal.\\n\\nThe upper vaginal wall, uterus and cervix also develop from the Mullerian ducts.\\n\\nDerivatives of the urogenital sinus:\\n\\n• Lower vagina\\n• Skene’s tubercles\\n• Bartholin’s gland\\n• Urinary bladder\\n• Urethra","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Circle of Willis does not get a contribution from","options":["Posterior cerebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior communicating artery"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Middle cerebral artery\\n\\nCircle of Willis does not get a contribution from the middle cerebral artery.\\n\\nThe circle of Willis is located in the interpeduncular fossa at the base of the brain. It is an anastomosis between the internal carotid arteries and vertebral arteries. It allows the blood entering from either artery to be distributed to both the cerebral hemispheres.\\n\\nThe arteries forming the circle of Willis are:\\n\\n• Anterior communicating artery\\n• Anterior cerebral arteries\\n• Internal carotid arteries\\n• Posterior communicating arteries\\n• Posterior cerebral arteries\\n• Basilar artery","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 24-year-old medical student received booster dose of Hepatitis B vaccination, following which she developed weakness in the abduction and lateral rotation of that arm. The lesion is most likely to be in which of the following nerves?","options":["Nerve of Bell","Labourer’s nerve","Musician\'s nerve","Surgeon’s nerve"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Surgeon’s nerve\\n\\nThe axillary nerve also called a Surgeon\'s nerve is frequently injured during inadvertent intramuscular (IM) injections.\\n\\nNamed nerves of the upper limb:\\n\\n• Laborer’s nerve: Median nerve.\\n• Musician’s nerve: Ulnar nerve.\\n• Surgeon’s nerve: Axillary nerve.\\n• Nerve of Bell: Long thoracic nerve.\\n\\nCourse of Axillary nerve:\\n\\n• Origin -\xa0\xa0Posterior cord of brachial plexus in the axilla.\\n• In Axilla - Exits the axilla\xa0through the quadrangular space to enter the arm.\\n• In Arm -\xa0Winds around the surgical neck of the humerus and gives cutaneous supply to the lower part of deltoid\xa0\\"upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm\\".\\n\\nAxillary nerve Injury:\\n\\nInjury mostly is due to fracture of\xa0surgical neck of humerus, inferior dislocation of humerus and inadvertent IM injection.\\n\\nTest: Loss of sensation over the skin of the lower part of the deltoid muscle (regimental badge anesthesia) in case of lesion of the nerve.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/848102a1019298160f80.jpg"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 5-month-old infant was brought to the pediatric OP for a routine checkup. The child had congenital hypoparathyroidism. CT scan revealed thyroid hypoplasia and absent thymus. Abnormality in the development of which of the following pharyngeal pouches will most likely cause the given features?","options":["Second and third pharyngeal pouch","First and second pharyngeal pouch","Fourth and fifth pharyngeal pouch","Third and fourth pharyngeal pouch"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Third and fourth pharyngeal pouch\\n\\nThe defect is most likely to be present in the development of third and fourth pharyngeal pouches as the inferior parathyroid gland and thymus develop from 3rd pouch while the superior parathyroid gland and parafollicular\xa0cells of the thyroid develop from 4th pouch.\\n\\nDerivatives of the Pharyngeal Pouches\\n\\n1\\n\\n• Tympanic cavity\xa0(middle ear cavity)\\n• Auditory tube\xa0(eustachian tube)\\n\\n2\\n\\n• Palatine tonsils\\n• Tonsillar fossa\\n\\n3\\n\\n• Inferior parathyroid glands (parathyroid III)\\n• Thymus\\n\\n4\\n\\n• Superior parathyroid glands (Parathyroid IV)\\n• Ultimobranchial body - gives rise to parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid gland","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/52ce4226038b2b4488d8.jpg"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 12-year-old presented with fever and difficulty swallowing. He had swelling in the marked region and was advised to undergo surgery. Post-surgery the gauze continued to soak with blood. Which of the following vessels must have been injured?","options":["Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Paratonsillar vein","Retromandibular vein","Ascending pharyngeal artery"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Paratonsillar vein\\n\\nThe given image is a cadaveric specimen with the arrow pointing towards, the tonsillar fossa,\xa0and the\xa0given clinical vignette is suggestive of\xa0tonsillitis. Post tonsillectomy hemorrhage is\xa0commonly venous in origin, from the paratonsillar/ external palatine vein.\\n\\nTorrential hemorrhage\xa0can be due\xa0to injury to an aberrantly large or aberrantly placed tonsillar arterial branch of the facial artery.\\n\\nBlood supply of the tonsils:\\n\\nArterial supply:\\n\\n• The tonsillar branch of the facial artery is the main supply.\\n• Ascending palatine branch of the facial artery.\\n• Ascending pharyngeal branch of the external carotid artery.\\n• Dorsal lingual branches of the lingual artery.\\n• Descending palatine branches of the maxillary artery.\\n\\nVenous supply:\\n\\n• Veins from the tonsils drain into\xa0paratonsillar\xa0vein which joins the common facial vein and pharyngeal venous plexus.\\n\\nTypes of Hemorrhage seen during/post tonsillectomy:\\n\\n• Primary\xa0hemorrhage:\\n\\n• Occurs\xa0during surgery\xa0due to injury to blood vessels.\\n• Can be controlled by\xa0pressure, ligation, or electrocoagulation of the bleeding vessels.\\n\\n• Reactionary\xa0hemorrhage:\\n\\n• It occurs\xa0within 24 hours of surgery.\\n• It may be due to\xa0dislodgement of the clot, slippage of ligature, or return of blood pressure to normal.\\n• It is managed by\xa0removal of clot and application of pressure or vasoconstrictor.\\n• If not controlled the patient is\xa0anesthetized\xa0and hemorrhage is\xa0managed by re-exploration.\\n\\n• Secondary\xa0hemorrhage:\\n\\n• It occurs between\xa024 hours to 10 days\xa0(most common between\xa05th\xa0to 8th\xa0postoperative days).\\n• It is due to\xa0sepsis and premature separation\xa0of the membrane.\\n• The patient is started on\xa0IV antibiotics\xa0and if not controlled, general\xa0anesthesia\xa0is given and the bleeding vessel is\xa0electrocoagulated\xa0or\xa0ligated.\\n\\nThe images below summarize the blood supply to the tonsils and the relations of the tonsillar bed respectively.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4aa28a85fd825aafe5a5.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c72d5fbee02a7234c733.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c34a2c2e06b57b44f452.jpg"}]},{"n":20,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Which part of this bone is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1\\n\\nThe part of the scapula marked as 1 is the coracoid process\xa0and it is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa.\xa0The tip of the coracoid process lies approximately 2.5 cm below the clavicle, immediately lateral to the infraclavicular fossa (deltopectoral triangle).\xa0Hence, the coracoid process can be palpated below the clavicle.\\n\\nThe coracoid process arises from the upper border of the neck of the scapula and it projects forwards and slightly laterally.\xa0Attachments at the coracoid process include\\n\\n• The conoid portion of the coracoclavicular ligament\\n• Pectoralis minor\\n• Medial end of coraco-acromial ligament\\n• Coracohumeral ligament\\n• Glenocorocoid ligament\\n• Coracobrachialis muscle\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption B - Acromian process continues from the spine of the scapula. The acromion is subcutaneous over its dorsal surface, The attachments at the acromion are the coracoacromial ligament, the articular capsule of the acromioclavicular joint, and horizontal fibers of the trapezius.\\n\\nOption C - The glenoid fossa is present at the lateral angle of the scapula. It is articular with the humerus to form the shoulder joint.\\n\\nOption D - The inferior angle lies over the seventh rib. Dorsally, it is related to latissimus dorsi. The angle is visible when the arm is raised over the head.\\n\\nParts of the scapula are given in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/fa61e7ecdf712c20ed75.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/18bda187a0f0eaa5dfc1.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Identify the structures which consist of commissural fibres:","options":["A, B, C, and D","A, B and C only","A and B only","D only"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A, B and C only\\n\\nThe correct answer is A, B, and C only.\\n\\nThe structure marked as \'D\' is the body of the fornix\xa0and it\xa0mainly contains\xa0association fibres. The commissure of the fornix (hippocampal commissure) contains the commissural fibers.\\n\\nA- Corpus callosum\\n\\nB- Anterior commissure\\n\\nC- Posterior commissure\\n\\nD- Body of the Fornix\\n\\nThe locations of the commissural fibers are:\\n\\n• Corpus callosum-\xa0Medial surface of the brain\\n• Anterior commissure-\xa0Beneath the rostrum of the corpus callosum\\n• Posterior commissure-\xa0In the inferior lamina of the pineal gland\\n• Habenular commissure-\xa0In the superior lamina of the pineal gland\\n• Hippocampal commissure-\xa0Between the right fornix and the left fornix\\n\\n(Note: Varied information regarding the fibers present in the Fornix. 41st edition Gray\'s Clinical anatomy mentions Fornix under association fibers, the commissure of the fornix under commissural fibers. Whereas, Snell\'s Neuroanatomy textbook mentions the Fornix under commissural fibers.)","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/665d40374538a78ed874.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b0a8f629025b352d2d3f.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6da6a1942a15f5723c7d.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/34abbd37f85dc70b4adf.gif"}]},{"n":22,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"The given area of the anterior surface of the left kidney is related to the ________________ :","options":["Jejunum","Hepatic flexure of the colon","Pancreas","Stomach"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Jejunum\\n\\nThe given area on the anterior surface of the left kidney is related to the jejunum.\\n\\nThe\xa0anterior relations\xa0of the\xa0right kidney\xa0include:\\n\\n• Right suprarenal gland\\n• Second/descending part of the duodenum\\n• Liver\\n• Hepatic flexure of the colon\\n• Small intestine\\n\\nThe\xa0anterior relations\xa0of the\xa0left kidney\xa0include:\\n\\n• Left suprarenal gland\\n• Stomach\\n• Pancreas\\n• Jejunum\\n• Spleen\\n• Splenic flexure and the beginning of the descending colon\\n\\nThe posterior relations of the kidneys are as follows:\\n\\n• Vessels (1):\xa0Subcostal vessels\\n• Ligaments (2):\xa0Medial and\xa0Lateral Arcuate ligaments\\n• Nerves (3):\xa0Subcostal, Iliohypogastric, and Ilioinguinal nerves\\n• Muscles (4):\xa0Diaphragm, Psoas major, Quadratus lumborum, and\xa0Transverses abdominis.\\n• The\xa0right kidney is related to the 12th\xa0rib\xa0and the left kidney is related to the 11th\xa0and 12th\xa0ribs","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/8050b3923ca20af5d700.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ac8d50669d2cf1af46d6.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e93ec8c0aa7459066e20.jpg"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"All of the following are true about the phrenic nerve except that :","options":["It is formed at the lateral border of scalenus anterior","The right phrenic nerve passes through the vena caval opening","It arises mainly from C4 spinal nerve","It is a purely motor nerve"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. It is a purely motor nerve\\n\\nAll of the following are true about the phrenic nerve except option A as it is a mixed nerve (sensory and motor nerve).\\n\\nEach phrenic nerve provides the sole\xa0motor supply to its hemidiaphragm.\xa0In its thoracic course, each phrenic nerve supplies\xa0sensory\xa0branches to the\xa0mediastinal pleura, fibrous pericardium, and parietal serous pericardium.\\n\\nThe phrenic nerve is a\xa0mixed nerve derived mostly from the\xa0C4 ramus\xa0and also receives contributions from the\xa0C3 and C5\xa0ventral rami.\\n\\nIt is formed at the lateral border of the scalenus anterior. At the root of the neck, the right phrenic nerve runs anterior to the second part of the\xa0right subclavian artery\xa0from which it is separated by\xa0scalenus anterior\xa0muscle. The phrenic nerve lies posterior to the right\xa0subclavian vein.\\n\\nThe right phrenic nerve is\xa0shorter\xa0and more vertical than the left. The\xa0right phrenic nerve\xa0passes through the\xa0vena caval opening.\xa0The\xa0left phrenic nerve\xa0passes\xa0through the\xa0muscular part\xa0of the diaphragm just\xa0anterior to\xa0the\xa0central tendon.\\n\\nThe phrenic nerve is responsible for the\xa0referred pain\xa0which\xa0usually occurs when there is inflammation of the diaphragmatic pleura, e.g. in basal pneumonia, pleural effusions, or with irritation of the peritoneal pleura, as with a subphrenic abscess. It\xa0is frequently felt at the\xa0tip\xa0of the\xa0shoulder\xa0due to the common root of origin of the phrenic nerve and the nerves supplying the shoulder.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ac8a2c6a9e46b9619d3e.jpg"}]},{"n":24,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"The diaphragmatic surface of the heart is shown below. Identify the structures marked X and Y.","options":["Posterior interventricular artery and small cardiac vein","Posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein","Right coronary artery and middle cardiac vein","Left coronary artery and small cardiac vein"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein\\n\\nThe structure marked X is the posterior interventricular artery and the structure marked Y is the middle cardiac vein which can be identified as it drains into the coronary sinus. Both these structures lie in the posterior interventricular sulcus.\\n\\nTributaries of coronary sinus\xa0include the following:\\n\\n• Great cardiac vein\\n• Middle cardiac vein\\n• Small cardiac vein\\n• Posterior vein of the left ventricle\\n• Oblique vein(of\xa0Marshall) of the left atrium","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/3aeafc1140689c8cba59.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2811d9a000ad351d3c4d.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6d169b5a0ea839ac7979.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"An elderly man is diagnosed with a slow-growing tumor in the deep perineal pouch. Which of the following is not a content of this structure?","options":["Bulbourethral gland","Sphincter urethrae","Dorsal nerve of penis","Root of penis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Root of penis\\n\\nThe root of the penis, which consists of two crus and one bulb, is the content of the superficial perineal pouch.\\n\\nThe deep perineal pouch is used to describe the space enclosed between the endopelvic fascia and the perineal membrane and includes the following contents:\\n\\nMales\\nCommon to both\\nFemales\\n\\n• Dorsal nerve of the penis\\n• Artery of the penis\\n• Origin\xa0 of the branches of the penile artery\\n• Bulbourethral glands\\n\\n• Urethra\\n• Deep transverse perinei\\n• Sphincter urethrae\\n• Origin of the urethral artery\\n\\n• Dorsal nerve of the clitoris\\n• Artery of clitoris\\n• Origin\xa0 of the branches of the artery of the clitoris","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A 55-year-old male presented with rigidity, frequent falls, slow movements, tremors, and a short stepping gait when he entered the doctor\'s room. Which of the following areas is most likely to be affected?","options":["D","B","C","A"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. C\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of Parkinson\'s disease, which is associated with the\xa0degeneration of substantia nigra (marked as \\"C\\").\\n\\nSubstantia nigra is pigmented due to the\xa0presence of neuromelanin granules,\xa0which help in the\xa0synthesis of dopamine.\xa0In Parkinson\'s disease,\xa0 there is an\xa0absence/reduced pigmentation of the substantia nigra.\\n\\nPostural instability along with bradykinesia, rigidity, and resting tremor are cardinal features of Parkinson\'s disease.\xa0Because of motor disability, it is also referred to as\xa0paralysis agitans.\\n\\nNotable gait abnormalities are:\\n\\n• Short stepped gait- parkinsonism\\n• Scissoring gait- spasticity i.e. UMN lesion\\n• High-stepped, slapping gait- posterior column or peripheral nerve disease\\n• Waddling gait- proximal muscle weakness\\n• Wide stepping gait - cerebellar dysfunction","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/ce4dca81b2bfad800de0.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c1f558a26e6f81ff5d8c.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"While evaluating a cyanotic newborn, his echocardiogram shows pulmonic stenosis involving the root of the pulmonary artery. Which developmental structure\'s defective maturation is most likely responsible for this condition?","options":["4","3","1","2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 4\\n\\nThis newborn is found to have pulmonic stenosis involving the root of the pulmonary artery. The structure\xa0that gives rise to the root of the pulmonary artery is the\xa0truncus arteriosus, marked\xa0as \'4\' in the given image.\\n\\nStructures marked:\\n\\n1. Sinus venosus\\n2. Atrium\\n3. Bulbus cordis\\n4. Truncus arteriosus\\n\\nThe heart tube begins to bend by day\xa023. This bending creates the cardiac loop which is completed by day 28. The fate of various structures formed as a result of this bending is as follows:\\n\\nGiven below are the heart tube and their derivatives:\\n\\nPart of the heart tube\\nDerivative structure in the heart\\n\\nBulbus cordis\\n\\n• Proximal 1/3\\n\\n• Middle 1/3 (conus cordis)\\n\\n• Distal 1/3 (truncus arteriosus)\\n\\n• Trabeculated part of right ventricle\\n\\n• Outflow tract of right and left ventricles\\n\\n• Root and proximal portions of aorta and pulmonary artery\\n\\nPrimitive ventricle\\n\\n• Trabeculated part of the left ventricle\\n\\nPrimitive atrium\\n\\n• The common atrium that splits to right and left atrium","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/5950aeedae3eefeddf12.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/8c3ce3f10cdc9179a80f.jpg"}]},{"n":28,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"A patient underwent surgery for the varicose vein. He now complains of sensory loss over the medial aspect of the leg and foot. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be injured?","options":["Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Saphenous nerve\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario in a patient with sensory loss over the medial aspect of the leg and foot is suggestive of injury to the saphenous nerve.\xa0Injury to the saphenous nerve is common following varicose vein surgery.\\n\\nThe saphenous nerve\xa0is the longest and largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve and is the longest nerve in the body. It arises from the posterior division of the femoral nerve. It then enters the adductor canal and exits it by piercing the fascia lata between the gracilis and sartorius and supplies the skin over the patella. The saphenous nerve then descends along the medial border of the tibia along with the great saphenous vein\xa0(anterior to the vein) and supplies the skin on the medial side of the\xa0knee, leg, and foot\xa0up to the first metatarsophalangeal joint.\\n\\nDuring stripping of veins in\xa0varicose surgery, injury to the saphenous nerve is common as it closely travels with the great saphenous vein. Sensory loss over the medial side of the knee, leg, and foot is observed in saphenous nerve injury.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Sural nerve injury causes sensory loss over the posterior and lateral aspects\xa0of the distal third of the leg and lateral side of the foot and fifth toe. It is often used as an autologous peripheral nerve graft as it is readily identified and easy to harvest.\\n\\nOption B: Superficial peroneal nerve injury causes sensory loss over the lateral aspect of the leg and the dorsum of the foot. It also causes weakness of foot eversion.\\n\\nOption C: Deep peroneal nerve injury causes weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension but normal eversion of the foot.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/42e8006919a77a8cef01.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/17d49bb03dd5d1705106.jpg"}]},{"n":29,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"Area marked \\"?\\" in the diagram below is ________.","options":["Hypoglossal triangle","Vestibular triangle","Vagal triangle","Facial colliculus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hypoglossal triangle\\n\\nThe image is of the floor of the 4th ventricle, and the area marked \\"?\\" is the hypoglossal triangle.\\n\\nThe floor of the 4th ventricle is diamond-shaped and lies on the dorsal surface of the pons and superior half of medulla.\\n\\nIt is divided into two symmetrical halves by the longitudinal median sulcus. Each half is further divided by sulcus limitans into a medial region,\xa0medial eminence, and a lateral region,\xa0vestibular area.\\n\\nThe vestibular nuclei lie beneath the vestibular area.\\n\\nAt the superior end of the sulcus limitans, a bluish-gray area is produced by a cluster of nerve cells containing melanin pigment called substantia ferruginea.\\n\\nAt the inferior end of the medial eminence, the fibers from the motor nucleus of the facial nerve looping over the abducens nucleus are represented by the facial colliculus.\\n\\nInferiorly, the medial eminence is represented by the hypoglossal triangle overlying the hypoglossal nucleus and the vagal triangle overlying the dorsal nucleus of vagus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4a29b5185ca5e59b7d44.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e790702120b4b540c425.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d5781c09de9cd6d60e3b.png"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Anatomy","text":"The muscle marked by the arrow in the image below is innervated by ________","options":["Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Subscapular nerve"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Dorsal scapular nerve\\n\\nThe muscle marked in the above image is levator scapulae, which is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve.\\n\\nThe appendicular group of back muscles and their innervations include:\\n\\nMuscle\\nNerve Supply\\n\\nTrapezius\\n\\nAccessory nerve\\n\\nLatissimus dorsi\\n\\nThoracodorsal nerve\\n\\nLevator scapulae\\n\\nDorsal scapular nerve\\n\\nRhomboid major and minor\\n\\nDorsal scapular nerve\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: The\xa0suprascapular nerve\xa0innervates the supra- and infraspinatus muscles.\\n\\nOption D: The subscapular nerves\xa0supply the teres major and subscapularis muscles.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/3d9b0f39ddb41112dc85.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a11ddabc3699778acf53.jpg"}]}]}')}}]);